        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe audit logic is in the wrong unit, and that placement also leaks it into a pipeline it doesn't belong to.\n\n**1. `SaveOwner` is shared by both create and update pipelines.**\n`SaveOwner` is wired as the `save` step in *both* `owners.POST.yml` (create) and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` (update). The spec scopes the audit line to \"On successful create\", but embedding it in `SaveOwner` means it now also fires on every PUT update. Beyond the behavioral leak, this is a structural smell: a reused persistence step is now carrying create-only concern.\n\n**2. Auditing is a distinct concern and belongs in its own step.**\nThis codebase is built on deliberately fine-grained single-responsibility functions \u2014 `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`, `CountOwnerNamesakes`, `NormalizeOwnerRegistrationDate`, `RejectCityAtCapacity`, etc. A maintainer here would expect audit emission to be its own step, not folded into `Save`. `SaveOwner`'s responsibility is persistence; mixing in logging makes it a mini catch-all and breaks the one-verb-per-class naming/altitude convention described in AGENTS.md.\n\n**Suggested shape:**\n- Add a new step class, e.g. `AuditOwnerCreated`, reading `@Val Owner` and emitting the AUDIT line (holding its own `Logger`).\n- Wire it only in `owners.POST.yml`, between `save` and `respond`:\n  ```yaml\n  save:\n    class: ...owner.SaveOwner\n    govern: [ transaction ]\n    next: audit\n  audit:\n    class: ...owner.AuditOwnerCreated\n    govern: [ transaction ]\n    next: respond\n  ```\n- Revert `SaveOwner` to its persistence-only form.\n\nThis keeps `SaveOwner` reusable and honest, scopes the audit to the create path per the spec, and matches the step-per-concern idiom the rest of the package follows."
      }
